  "client_view_text": " This is for an embedded system using SQLite, but the idea applies to any database that uses a write-ahead log.\nAn embedded system using SQLite must be able to recover its database after a crash, in case there was a write or series of writes in progress at the time of the crash. A write-ahead log (WAL) provides this capability. The WAL is a file that stores the entire write history of the database, including all the writes that are still in progress. This means that when the system is restarted (after a crash, for example), the WAL can be replayed from the moment that the crash occurred, ensuring that the database is left in a consistent state.\nThe WAL file contains a series of transactions, each of which includes the original data, the modified data, and any necessary operations to perform the update. When the system restarts, the WAL file is replayed, and each transaction is re-applied to the database, effectively \"undoing\" any partial writes left in the database at the time of the crash. This ensures that the database is always consistent, even in the presence of a crash.\nThanks for pointing out the unclear part. I'll make sure to expand on this in the next paragraph.\nIn other words, when the system crashes,"
}